    "Year 12 - Story 1: The Competition"

    "It’s been a while."
    "The little girl who used to stand on her toes to peek over the counter had grown taller."
    "Her hair was longer now, falling past her shoulders when she forgot to tie it back."
    "Her arms and legs had stretched faster than she expected, and sometimes she still bumped into tables because she had not quite learned where her body ended."
    "She was no longer the tiny child who hid behind her mother at the door of Home Plate."
    "At twelve years old, Stella walked through town by herself."
    "And today, she looked absolutely miserable."
    "She moved along the road with her school bag hanging from one shoulder."
    "Her face was heavy, and her steps were slow."
    "Every few seconds, she kicked a small stone ahead of her, watched it bounce across the ground, then caught up with it and kicked it again."
    "The stone was not responsible for anything, but Stella kicked it anyway."
    "That afternoon, her school had held its annual spaceship model contest."
    "Every student had been given several weeks to design and build a model of any kind of spacecraft they wanted."
    "It could be a cargo ship, a scout vessel, racing shuttles, long-range exploration craft."
    "Anything, as long as it was made by hand and could be displayed in the school hall."
    "Stella knew she was not the best at building things."
    "Her hands were not bad, exactly."
    "But they did not always listen to the version of the idea inside her head."
    "Still, she had signed up, of course she had signed up."
    "Her father had once worked on ships that travelled farther than most people ever dreamed of going."
    "Stella had grown up hearing stories about engine rooms, launch platforms, strange planets, and skies with too many moons."
    "So when she saw the contest notice pinned outside the classroom, something inside her stood up very straight."
    "I can do it too."
    "She had spent weeks preparing, borrowed books from the school library."
    "She searched for pictures of old exploration ships."
    "She measured pieces of cardboard, cut plastic sheets, glued fins onto the sides, and painted the hull three separate times because the first two versions looked wrong."
    "Her parents had been delighted when she told them."
    "Neither of them built it for her, they knew Stella would hate that."
    "But they listened when she talked, and sometimes they helped her find better materials."
    "For a while, that made Stella feel almost certain."
    "She did not need to be the best immediately, she just needed to make something that showed she had tried."
    "But then contest day came, and everyone brought their ships."
    "Some of them were fine, some leaned strangely to one side."
    "One boy's shuttle lost a wing before lunch."
    "Those made Stella feel better, but then she saw the others."
    "Some models looked impossibly clean."
    "The edges were smooth, the panels fit together neatly, and the paint looked even."
    "One ship had tiny lights inside its cockpit."
    "Another had landing gear that actually folded."
    "Someone had built a silver cruiser so detailed that Stella could see individual window frames along the side."
    "Stella stared at it for a long time."
    "It looked less like a school project and more like something from a professional display case."
    "She was almost sure parts of it had been 3D printed."
    "But of course, nobody said that, and the judges did not seem very interested in asking."
    "They smiled, they praised the details, and wrote things down on their little sheets."
    "Stella stood beside her own model and tried not to look at the slightly uneven paint near the left wing."
    "Her ship was not terrible."
    "It had a good shape."
    "The colour was nice, the nose section even looked close to what she had imagined."
    "But next to the others, it looked small."
    "Handmade."
    "In the worst possible way."
    "In the end, Stella did not win."
    "She was not last, she even received a participation certificate with a small printed star in the corner."
    "Her teacher said she should be proud."
    "Stella said thank you."
    "She folded the certificate once, then she put it into her bag where she would not have
    to look at it."
    "After school, most of the other students left in little groups, laughing and talking about the winning models."
    "Stella did not walk home."
    "She knew her parents would ask how it went."
    "They would not be angry, and that was the problem."
    "They would be kind, and tell her she had worked hard."
    "They would say one contest did not decide anything."
    "And Stella did not want to hear that yet."
